mode: "100644"
text: "# TikTok Mechanics — the discovery engine\n\nTikTok is the most discovery-driven platform there is. Understanding *how* the For You Page (FYP)\ndecides what to push is the whole game — and it's different enough from Reels that a Reel reposted\nto TikTok usually underperforms.\n\n> Specs, lengths, and features change fast — re-verify quarterly. The principles are durable.\n\n## Content-first, not follower-first\n\nThe FYP shows content based on **how people respond to it**, not on how many followers you have. A\nzero-follower account can hit a million views; a big account can flop. TikTok tests every video on a\nsmall audience, watches the signals, and expands reach if they're strong. This is why TikTok is the\nbest platform for reach from a standing start — and why **the video itself has to earn it every\ntime**.\n\n## The signals that expand reach\n\nIn rough order of weight:\n\n1. **Watch time / completion rate** — did people watch to the end? The single biggest signal.\n2. **Rewatches / loops** — did they watch again? Hugely powerful; design for it.\n3. **Shares** — sent to a friend / off-platform. The strongest \"this is worth spreading\" signal.\n4. **Comments** — especially ones that spark replies.\n5. **Saves**, follows, likes.\n\nEverything in `hooks-and-retention.md` exists to maximize the first two.\n\n## The brutal hook window\n\nTikTok users scroll fast — you have **~1 second**, and the first ~3 seconds decide the video. If the\nopening doesn't stop the scroll, nothing else matters. There is no warm-up, no \"hey guys, welcome\nback,\" no logo intro. The hook is verbal + on-screen text + visual, all at once. (See\n`hooks-and-retention.md`.)\n\n## Make TikToks, not ads\n\nTikTok's own advice, and it's true: **native, authentic, slightly raw content outperforms polished,\nad-like content.** The platform's culture rewards a real person talking to a phone camera over a\nglossy brand spot. Over-produced content reads as an ad and gets scrolled. This is the single most\ncommon brand mistake on TikTok — and the core pushback this skill makes.\n\n## Sound is central\n\nTikTok is a sound-on platform built around audio. Trending sounds, original audio, and music are\nwoven into discovery — using a rising sound can give a video an early lift, and a great original\naudio can itself become a trend. But a sound has to *fit* (see `formats-and-sound.md`); forcing an\nirrelevant trend is worse than using none.\n\n## TikTok is a search engine\n\nIncreasingly, people (especially younger users) **search on TikTok** like Google. Videos surface\nfor search queries and in \"Others searched for\" prompts. So TikTok SEO matters: say your **keywords\nout loud**, put them in **on-screen text**, and in the **caption** — TikTok reads all three.\nKeyword + niche hashtags beat generic high-volume tags.\n\n## Length\n\n- **Short (≈7–34s)** still dominates for FYP virality and rewatches — easiest to complete and loop.\n- **Longer (1–3 min+)** works for storytime, tutorials, and search-driven content; TikTok supports\n  ~10 min recorded in-app and up to ~60 min via upload (verify-quarterly). TikTok has nudged toward\n  longer content, but short remains the safest bet for reach.\n- **Let the idea pick the length** — never pad to fill time; dead air kills completion.\n\n## Native features (use where they fit)\n\n- **Stitch / Duet** — react to or build on another video (great for reach + commentary).\n- **Green screen** — talk over an image/article (explainers, reactions).\n- **CapCut** — the de-facto editor; trending templates, captions, transitions.\n- **Auto-captions** — always on (accessibility + sound-off viewers); still add intentional on-screen\n  text for the hook and keywords.\n\n## Cadence & disclosure\n\n- **Consistency over bursts** — regular posting feeds the algorithm more tests; treat timing as\n  general guidance, not the user's best time (WoopSocial post analytics are lifetime counts, not a timing model).\n- **AI-disclosure** — WoopSocial auto-discloses AI-generated media on TikTok; still follow TikTok's\n  synthetic-media rules and the brand's policy.\n"
